WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.720
It's hard to anticipate what might happen
on Game of Thrones, especially now that the

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.200
HBO show has bypassed its source material.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:11.940
The eighth and final season of Thrones is
no exception to this rule of unpredictability,

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.240
but the most recent episode may have actually
spoiled the finale.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.800
A melancholy song Lady Brienne's squire Podrick,
played by Daniel Portman, sings during "A

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.420
Knight of the Seven Kingdoms" seems to foreshadow
which person vying for the Iron Throne will

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.200
end up ruling.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.220
Warning: Spoilers ahead.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.860
The musical moment happens while Podrick,
Brienne, Jaime Lannister, Tormund Giantsbane,

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.800
Tyrion Lannister, and Ser Davos Seaworth are
gathered around a fire, awaiting the arrival

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.620
of the Night King and the Army of the Dead.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:45.020
They drink and reminisce on past battles on
what could be the last night of their lives.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.300
When the wine runs out, Tyrion requests someone
sing a song.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.320
No one but Podrick pipes up, and he begins
singing a mournful tune:

00:00:52.700 --> 00:01:01.460
"High in the halls of the kings who are gone
/ Jenny would dance with her ghosts"

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.960
He continues, with lyrics about how Jenny
couldn't remember the ghosts' names, but they

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:09.380
took away her sorrow and pain by spinning
her on an old stone.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.380
The song then repeats that she "never wanted
to leave."

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:17.240
This scene did more than just tug on heartstrings
as it played over shots of our heroes in Winterfell

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.740
readying themselves for battle.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.450
It evoked the mood Pippin created when he
sang "The Edge of Night" ahead of the Battle

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.560
of Osgiliath in The Lord of the Rings.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.439
In the A Song of Ice and Fire books, this
tune is titled "Jenny's Song," and is directly

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.480
connected to the series' central characters
and their ancestors.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:37.880
"Jenny's Song" appears in the novels as one
of the Ghost of High Heart's favorite songs,

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.220
which she refers to as "my Jenny's song."

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:45.720
Jenny is Jenny of Oldstones, a low-status
woman who married Prince Duncan Targaryen.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.869
Their union, which Prince Duncan's father,
King Aegon V, disapproved of, spurred Prince

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:54.480
Duncan to renounce his claim to the Iron Throne
despite being the rightful heir.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.140
In the same way Jaime Lannister shoved Bran
Stark out of a Winterfell tower to protect

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:03.840
his incestuous relationship with his sister
Cersei, Prince Duncan made the bold decision

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.960
to abdicate the throne to marry Jenny.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:11.080
Not only does Jenny and Duncan's story mirror
that of Jaime and Cersei's, it it also parallels

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:16.700
two other Targaryens: Jon Snow, whose real
name is Aegon Targaryen, and Daenerys.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:21.400
In episode 2, Jon reveals to his lady love
that he's actually her nephew, the son of

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.060
her brother Rhaegar Targaryen and Lyanna Stark.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.959
That makes him the rightful heir to the Iron
Throne, given he's a living male descendent

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.940
of Aerys "The Mad King" Targaryen.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.040
Podrick singing "Jenny's Song" just before
that conversation happens might foreshadow

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.780
the events of the Game of Thrones finale:
Jon could relinquish his power to be with

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.300
Daenerys, just as Prince Duncan did.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:46.880
It's doubtful that Podrick knew everything
about Jon's parentage and the story of Jenny

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:50.770
and Duncan when he decided to sing the sad
tune, but there's even more hidden meaning

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:55.060
in the song that supports the idea Jon may
give up the Iron Throne to be with Daenerys.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.440
"You didn't tell me you were going to abandon
your crown."

00:02:58.440 --> 00:02:59.900
"I never wanted a crown."

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:04.590
The Ghost of High Heart is thought to be the
first person who delivered the prophecy that

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:09.550
Aerys and Rhaella Targaryen, Dany's parents
and Jon's grandparents, would be the ancestors

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.100
of the "Prince That Was Promised."

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:16.020
Rhaegar, Dany's brother, grew obsessive over
the prophecy, believing he was the Prince

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.020
That Was Promised.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:20.180
He thought that if he wasn't the Prince, one
of his children would be.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:23.980
As Vanity Fair notes, Rhaegar may have actually
written "Jenny's Song."

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:28.460
In the books, during the Tourney of Harrenhal,
he plays a song for his love Lyanna Stark,

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:29.520
Jon Snow's mother.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.220
It's not out of the realm of possibility that
the tune was the one Podrick sang.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:38.440
Just after Podrick sings "Jenny's Song," Jon
and Daenerys stand in front of Lyanna Stark's

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.470
statue in the Winterfell crypts.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.540
Daenerys says about Rhaegar:

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.820
"Everyone told me he was decent and kind.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:47.980
He liked to sing."

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.151
There stands a chance that "Jenny's Song"
is more about the Prince that Was Promised

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:53.800
prophecy than Jenny herself.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:58.680
It tells the story of the true-born heir to
the Iron Throne giving up everything for love.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:03.520
Jon, Rhaegar's son, might be both the Prince
That Was Promised and someone who, like his

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.360
ancestor Duncan, surrenders his claim to kingship.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.400
There really is no telling whether "Jenny's
Song" and its many meanings reflect how the

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.910
series will conclude, or whether Jon will
actually choose love over power, but there's

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.359
a chance that the tune hints at what will
happen.

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:21.949
If it winds up that "Jenny's Song" is nothing
more than a tune sung at an emotional time,

00:04:21.949 --> 00:04:25.780
at least Daniel Portman got to prove he has
the voice of an angel.

